00:04:05.609 --> 00:04:07.650
So today we launched Cloud
Functions for Firebase

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:09.021
in a public beta.

00:04:09.021 --> 00:04:11.520
We've been working very closely
with the Cloud Platform team

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.860
on this for quite some time.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.350
Cloud Functions for Firebase
provides a first class

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.899
experience for Firebase
developers, built directly

00:04:18.899 --> 00:04:21.269
on top of Google
Cloud Functions.

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:23.700
So this actually has been
the number one most requested

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:25.950
feature from Firebase
developers and we're

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:28.020
very excited to talk
to you about it today.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:34.590
So Cloud Functions for Firebase
is our programmatic glue.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:37.500
It lets you extend and connect
Firebase and Cloud products

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.400
to create a completely
serverless mobile back

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.490
end for your apps.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:44.190
Cloud Functions are event
driven, lightweight JavaScript

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:46.920
functions that are deployed
to Google's Cloud which

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.820
are executed in a managed
Node.js environment.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.730
So Cloud Functions for Firebase
provides Firebase developers

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:56.520
with a dedicated SDK,
tight integration

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.200
with Firebase
tooling, as well as

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.817
additional Firebase
specific event providers.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:05.950
So how does it work?

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:09.400
So as I mentioned earlier, Cloud
Functions are event driven.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:10.900
You specify the
event your function

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:12.730
should be triggered by.

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:15.550
So first, an event is emitted
from an event provider, which

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:18.040
I'll go over in a little bit.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:20.770
If these conditions are met,
your function will be triggered

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.530
and a new instance
will be spun up.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:26.480
Then the code within your
function will be executed.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:28.180
Now within this code,
you can make calls

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:31.300
to other services and
APIs and as a result,

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:33.010
you can choose to
invoke other functions

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:37.930
since some of these services may
actually emit events as well.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:39.640
So after your code is
finished executing,

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:42.400
your function will
spin down and you only

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.640
pay for what you use
with Cloud Functions.

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:50.080
So let's quickly jump
back to Frank's app

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:52.180
and see how he can
actually resize images now

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:54.940
that he has Cloud Functions.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:56.779
First, you'll notice
that he no longer

00:05:56.779 --> 00:05:58.570
has to deal with setting
up his own servers

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:00.460
and a custom back end.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:03.220
His app can now communicate
directly to Cloud storage

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:05.089
using the Firebase SDKs.

00:06:05.089 --> 00:06:06.880
So this will give him
all the added benefit

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:09.420
that we talked about earlier.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.450
So now Frank can
create a Cloud Function

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:13.400
to resize images
that'll be triggered

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.500
when any image is
uploaded to Cloud

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:19.340
Storage at a specific bucket.

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:21.645
So he won't need to set up
and manage his own servers,

00:06:21.645 --> 00:06:24.020
he won't need to worry about
manually scaling his service

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:26.060
to meet the demands
of Hipster Chat,

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:29.060
and Frank can have a
completely serverless back end

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:31.407
for his mobile app
using Cloud Functions.

00:06:31.407 --> 00:06:32.990
And he can still do
everything that he

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:34.580
wants quickly and easily.

00:06:37.672 --> 00:06:39.088
And that makes
Frank really happy.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.619
So Cloud Functions
for Firebase has

00:06:44.619 --> 00:06:46.410
support from many
different event providers

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.184
from Firebase as well as Cloud.

00:06:49.184 --> 00:06:51.600
These integrations allow you
to extend their functionality

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:53.640
through Cloud Functions.

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.230
So for instance,
real time database

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:57.720
lets you trigger a function
when a write occurs

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:00.716
at a particular path
in the database.

00:07:00.716 --> 00:07:02.340
The Firebase
authentication integration

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.690
lets you trigger a function
whenever a new user is created

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:08.710
or whenever a new
user is deleted.

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:10.470
The Firebase
analytics integration

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:13.060
lets you trigger a function
whenever a new conversion

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:17.210
event is [? logged. ?]
Cloud Storage for Firebase

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:19.970
lets you trigger a function
when a storage object changes

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:22.330
within a bucket.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:24.760
Cloud Pub/Sub lets
you trigger a function

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:29.270
when a new message is received
in a particular Pub/Sub topic.

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:32.967
HTTPS functions all you to
expose a unique secure URL that

00:07:32.967 --> 00:07:34.800
can be triggered whenever
a request is made.

