00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:42.290
So, that was a
basic Hello World.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:46.280
Frank wants to actually
say hello to the world.

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:50.120
Frank has a dream of uniting
hipsters all around the globe,

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:53.630
but the world speaks
many different languages.

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:55.610
Frank wants his chat
app to let people

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:58.886
communicate with each other
in their own language.

00:10:58.886 --> 00:11:01.650
But how can we do this?

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:04.650
Let's start by looking
at his mobile app.

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:07.540
So far, Frank's app
is pretty simple.

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:10.620
There's no chat rooms,
there's just a messages node.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:12.840
Each message has a
unique ID and there's

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:15.360
a standard field for the text.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:18.720
You can find out how to
write this app and much more

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:22.890
for iOS, Android, and the open
web on our YouTube video Zero

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:25.100
to App.

00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:29.110
But what if we made our app
a little bit more complex?

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:32.800
What if the database already
had translations in it?

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:36.640
Well now we can see that can
very trivially update the UI

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:40.000
so that only renders the message
that's in the user's preferred

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.030
language.

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:44.160
But of course, the
big question then

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:47.820
is how do we get the
database in this state?

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:53.100
Well, Google has two great
products that Frank can use.

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:55.500
There's the Firebase real-time
database that he's already

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:58.330
using and Google Translate.

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:01.020
Now Translate does
have an API, but it's

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:03.600
meant for server side use.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:07.070
So how can Frank use this API
without including a server

00:12:07.070 --> 00:12:09.212
key inside his mobile app?

00:12:09.212 --> 00:12:10.670
Well we're here at
this talk today,

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:14.570
so I'm assuming you can guess
that it's Cloud Functions.

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:16.460
He'll write a
Cloud Function that

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:21.500
listens to the database
at messages/ID/message.

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:24.810
Now notice the ID here
is in curly brackets.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:26.600
This is one of my
favorite features

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:28.820
of our integration with
the real-time database

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:31.130
because that's a wildcard.

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:33.320
That means that we will
match on both the left

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:35.810
and the right side
of that wildcard

00:12:35.810 --> 00:12:39.980
and we'll capture the actual
value as an event parameter.

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:42.992
This means that a single
Cloud Function has the power

00:12:42.992 --> 00:12:44.450
to listen to events
that would have

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:49.870
taken thousands and thousands
of listeners before today.

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:51.800
Once our Cloud
Function is started,

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:53.800
we're going to make a few
requests to the Google

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:55.600
Translate API.

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:58.210
First we're going to ask for
the list of currently supported

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:00.910
languages and then
we're going to ask

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:05.530
for that message be translated
in each of those languages.

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:08.110
As we get results back,
we'll stream them back

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:10.880
to the Firebase
real-time database.

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:13.006
So let's code it up
and see it in action.

00:13:13.006 --> 00:13:16.795
Can we switch to
the laptop please?

00:13:16.795 --> 00:13:18.190
Cool.

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:22.520
So because Cloud Functions is
a Vanilla Node mod-- or Node

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:24.462
client, we can use Node modules.

00:13:24.462 --> 00:13:26.670
You can benefit from the
community of Node developers

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:27.930
out there.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:31.172
Now our first demo was
written in Vanilla JavaScript.

00:13:31.172 --> 00:13:32.880
I'm going to start
writing my demos using

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:34.588
some more advanced
features that are part

00:13:34.588 --> 00:13:37.050
of the upcoming JavaScript
7 standard, so I'm going

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:38.970
to use TypeScript to get them.

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:42.840
This will also give me syntax
complete and syntax error

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:45.320
checking.

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:49.490
So first, let's go ahead
and add that Translate API.

00:13:57.230 --> 00:13:59.360
So the Google Cloud
Translate library

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:02.570
returns a function that you
should call to initialize it.

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:04.880
I don't have to use
any parameters here.

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:07.280
Because Cloud Functions
is part of the Cloud

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:09.830
Platform and all
Cloud SDKs can auto

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:12.590
initialize inside
Cloud Platform.

00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:14.090
This is very useful
for making sure

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:17.640
that your app can use the same
code for your development,

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:20.300
testing, and production
environments.

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:23.030
Now let's write a Cloud Function
that listens to the database.

00:14:28.354 --> 00:14:29.810
There we go.

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:32.330
So you'll notice we have a
number of namespaces here.

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:34.820
These are all different
services that we can use

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:38.605
Cloud Functions to respond to.

00:14:38.605 --> 00:14:39.980
And a database
will let us listen

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:42.746
to changes on references.

00:14:42.746 --> 00:14:44.140
The code almost writes itself.

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:50.180
And we want to have
a write handler.

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:56.111
Now you'll notice because
of TypeScript-- whoops.

00:14:58.640 --> 00:14:59.590
There we go.

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:01.470
That we have, if
you can read this,

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:05.020
a function that takes an event
of delta snapshot and returns

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:05.680
anything.

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:08.096
But there's a hint that it
could be a promise of anything,

00:15:08.096 --> 00:15:10.410
and we'll get to
that in a second.

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:17.130
So, I'm going to use
an async handler which

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:20.340
is part of the upcoming
JavaScript 7 features.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:22.350
And first do something
with my event.

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:28.926
So you'll notice that
events have many fields.

00:15:28.926 --> 00:15:31.050
These are standard fields
available for every event

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:32.299
you handle in Cloud Functions.

00:15:32.299 --> 00:15:34.440
They include what
type of event was

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:37.290
triggered, what resource
fired that event,

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:40.840
and even the parameters that
are the actual values of that

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:43.320
wildcard up here.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:46.710
The data for each event though
depends on the kind of event

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:48.210
you fired.

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:50.395
For a database event,
it's a delta snapshot.

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:55.520
If you've used the Firebase
SDK for the real-time database,

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:58.030
you're very familiar
with data snapshots.

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:03.520
A delta snapshot is everything
a data snapshot is and more.

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:06.405
So next, let's go ahead and
get the set of translations

00:16:06.405 --> 00:16:08.280
or the set of languages
available from Google

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:09.178
Translate.

00:16:13.570 --> 00:16:16.420
This function can either take
a callback or return a promise.

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:18.580
And I'm going to
return a promise here.

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:21.590
I can either say then
and continue that way.

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:25.840
But since I'm using TypeScript,
I can just say await.

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:29.940
Now code will continue
after that's completed.

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:33.950
The Google Translate libraries
return an array of two values.

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:36.430
The first is a simplified
value and the second

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:39.070
is the raw values
from the REST API.

00:16:39.070 --> 00:16:40.960
Since I only want
the simple values,

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:42.280
I'll do this to extract that.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:46.685
Cool.

00:16:46.685 --> 00:16:48.310
Now that we have our
list of languages,

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:50.950
which are over 100
supported languages,

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:53.500
I want to fire than
more than 100 network

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:56.124
requests off at the same time.

00:16:56.124 --> 00:16:58.540
I'm going to need to eventually
keep track of that work so

00:16:58.540 --> 00:17:01.450
let's create a temporary.

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:08.265
For each language, I
want to do something.

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:26.200
And because it has that array,
I will extract the first volume.

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:32.130
So now we have over 100 requests
running at the same time

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:35.144
and I will take that value and
save it back to the database.

00:17:35.144 --> 00:17:37.560
I'm going to need a reference
to the database, which comes

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:38.730
as part of every snapshot.

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:42.250
There's a dot ref that is
where that snapshot came from.

00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:47.722
Now this reference here
is to this message node.

00:17:47.722 --> 00:17:49.180
If I want to make
a peer, I'm going

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:51.910
to go up one level and then
back down to the language code.

00:17:57.490 --> 00:18:02.810
And set that to the translation.

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:05.190
Now with an async function,
I can either await the result

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:05.940
or just return it.

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:12.229
Now all my work
has been complete.

00:18:12.229 --> 00:18:14.520
I just want to make sure that
my Cloud Function doesn't

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:17.460
terminate until all
the requests are done.

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:22.320
So I can create a promise
with promise dot all,

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:25.990
and that will wrap up
everything that's outstanding.

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:28.390
By returning that
promise, Cloud Functions

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:30.640
knows that I have
background work

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:32.770
and it will keep the
Cloud Function alive

00:18:32.770 --> 00:18:35.470
until that work completes.

00:18:35.470 --> 00:18:37.560
So let's try this
out in production.

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:41.210
It's the moment of truth.

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:44.150
So I needed to have
a write that matches

00:18:44.150 --> 00:18:47.280
messages and some value.

00:18:55.169 --> 00:18:56.960
This one can take a
little bit like I said,

00:18:56.960 --> 00:18:58.910
there is over 200
requests in here.

00:18:58.910 --> 00:19:02.054
100 languages, 100 writes
back to the Firebase database,

00:19:02.054 --> 00:19:02.720
but there we go.

00:19:07.590 --> 00:19:13.074
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:13.074 --> 00:19:14.490
So let's switch
back to the slides

00:19:14.490 --> 00:19:18.350
and find out what's
on Frank's list.

00:19:18.350 --> 00:19:21.430
So, Franks loves
his users and wants

00:19:21.430 --> 00:19:24.350
to make them feel
welcome in Hipster Chat.

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:26.990
He hears that bots
are so hot right now.

00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:30.490
And so he decides to write
his own, Mr. Mustachio.

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:33.370
Mr. Mustachio's
first task is going

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:37.480
to greet new users in
the general chat room.

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:41.080
Well luckily, Firebase
authentication also

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:43.630
works with Cloud Functions.

00:19:43.630 --> 00:19:46.730
It supports two kinds of
events, the creation of a user

00:19:46.730 --> 00:19:49.160
or deletion of a user.

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:50.900
Deletion events are
great for things

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:53.870
like clearing out the
profile information of a user

00:19:53.870 --> 00:19:55.730
when they leave your app.

00:19:55.730 --> 00:19:58.430
But today we're going to
use a user creation event

00:19:58.430 --> 00:20:00.930
to give our bot some life.

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:03.541
So let's switch back
to the [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:03.541 --> 00:20:06.270
There we go.

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:09.030
So in this demo, I'm going to
use the Firebase Admin SDK.

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:15.570
This is the SDK that
we've written to help

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:17.239
you build a server side app.

00:20:17.239 --> 00:20:19.530
And of course we've been
thinking about Cloud Functions

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:22.020
the entire time.

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:26.607
To initialize the SDK, we
need to call initialize app.

00:20:26.607 --> 00:20:28.440
And to do that, we're
going to use a feature

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:29.820
called functions.config.

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:39.630
Functions.config is a snapshot
of Google runtime config

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:42.120
from the time that
your app was deployed.

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:46.665
This is a great way to make
sure that your code stays--

00:20:46.665 --> 00:20:49.770
or and your config
stay cleanly separated.

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:53.707
This is a great way to make sure
that your apps stay testable.

00:20:53.707 --> 00:20:55.290
And we've added a
special variableness

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:58.080
config called Firebase.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:00.090
This is automatically
populated and has

00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:02.400
everything you need to
initialize a Firebase app.

00:21:10.910 --> 00:21:14.020
So we're going to create a
new Cloud Function, this time

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:16.095
integrating with Firebase Auth.

00:21:16.095 --> 00:21:18.220
We're going to listen to
a user event, specifically

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:19.510
the user creation event.

00:21:23.160 --> 00:21:26.130
Now this time, the event
data is a user record,

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:29.360
which makes sense because a
user record is what is returned

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:30.620
when you call create user.

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:35.840
Now in order to write
a message, we're

00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:38.780
going to need to push a new
value into our database.

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:46.730
Here, we just have the text
of the most interesting part.

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:49.450
We're using the display
name from that user record

00:21:49.450 --> 00:21:51.820
to make sure we greet the user.

00:21:51.820 --> 00:21:53.950
The rest of this
here is part of our--

00:21:53.950 --> 00:21:56.230
how we actually inform
our UX to make sure

00:21:56.230 --> 00:21:58.690
Mr. Mustachio is
visible as a bot.

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:02.935
So let's see it in production.

00:22:10.419 --> 00:22:11.710
And watch how fast it was, too.

00:22:19.604 --> 00:22:20.770
Say let's go back to slides.

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:26.170
So some time has passed.

00:22:26.170 --> 00:22:28.160
Hipster Chat has been growing.

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:31.600
It has so many users
in so many rooms

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:34.780
that Frank now needs
to add a feature

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:36.880
to let people get
others' attention

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:40.840
if they're subscribed to a room
but not currently reading it.

00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:44.720
So he's going to
add @here mentions.

00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:46.730
Frank does a little
bit of research

00:22:46.730 --> 00:22:49.610
and he knows about
Firebase Cloud Messaging.

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:51.200
It's a cross-platform
product that

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:54.800
lets you send notifications
across iOS, Android,

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:56.340
and the open web.

00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:58.490
But there's only one problem.

00:22:58.490 --> 00:23:00.230
He looks at the
quickstart sample

00:23:00.230 --> 00:23:03.170
and sees that there's a stubbed
out message called send token

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:05.250
to server.

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:07.270
That's because
until today, you've

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:10.730
needed a custom server in order
to be able to programmatically

00:23:10.730 --> 00:23:12.800
send notifications.

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:15.100
But not anymore.

00:23:15.100 --> 00:23:16.560
See, Frank is clever.

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:18.990
He knows that the
Firebase database has

00:23:18.990 --> 00:23:21.120
very flexible security rules.

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:23.460
He can create private
areas in the database that

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:26.550
are restricted to each user.

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:30.090
And so he's going to implement
his send token to server method

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:33.960
so that it pushes a device
token in an area that

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:36.540
is based off of the user ID.

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:39.976
Now he has secure storage
for all his device tokens

00:23:39.976 --> 00:23:41.850
and he can let Cloud
Functions do some magic.

00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:48.720
Now we're going to break our
push back end into two parts.

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:52.080
Rather than looking up every
device token for every user

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:53.330
that's in the topic--

00:23:53.330 --> 00:23:55.936
or in the room whenever
you get an @here,

00:23:55.936 --> 00:23:57.810
we're going to have a
subscription management

00:23:57.810 --> 00:24:01.320
piece in the part that
actually does the sending.

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:03.270
This is the first part.

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:05.130
We're going to
listen to the change

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:08.610
of all users in all rooms.

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:12.870
Whenever we notice that a user
has joined or left a room,

00:24:12.870 --> 00:24:17.550
we're going to subscribe or
unsubscribe from an FCM topic

00:24:17.550 --> 00:24:20.470
respectively.

00:24:20.470 --> 00:24:23.580
This will dramatically simplify
our push code because now we

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:25.380
can just scan messages.

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:28.020
If they contain
an app mention, we

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:32.060
can send a push notification
based on a topic corresponding

00:24:32.060 --> 00:24:32.880
to the target room.

00:24:35.540 --> 00:24:37.560
So, let's jump
back to some code.

00:24:40.970 --> 00:24:43.070
To bump the difficulty
one more time,

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:45.894
this demo is actually going
to use raw HTTP requests.

00:24:45.894 --> 00:24:47.560
So I'm going to add
yet another library.

00:24:55.530 --> 00:24:58.610
So, that first function
was a database function.

00:25:03.630 --> 00:25:07.990
I said for every room
wildcard, every user wildcard,

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:10.330
we're going to
listen to any write.

00:25:10.330 --> 00:25:13.660
Now that could have been a
user insertion or deletion,

00:25:13.660 --> 00:25:19.860
so we're going to find out
which and map it to the actions

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:23.160
that Cloud Messaging
supports, either a batch add

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:25.580
or a batch remove.

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:28.940
Next, we're going to
need the list of devices

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:32.950
that are associated
with this user account.

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:35.710
So we're going to use
the Admin SDK again.

00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:39.720
We're going to look up the
values in that private storage.

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:42.720
Now it's possible a user doesn't
actually have any tokens.

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:45.090
For example on
iOS, the user might

00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:47.380
have disabled
notifications altogether.

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:48.630
So let's check for that first.

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:55.020
If the snapshot does not
exist, the user has no tokens,

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:57.720
we'll log a debug
message and exit.

00:25:57.720 --> 00:25:59.910
Otherwise, we'll log a
different debug message

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:01.820
about how we're adding
or removing tokens.

00:26:05.060 --> 00:26:07.150
Oops, jumped ahead of myself.

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:09.550
So since we're
making HTTP requests,

00:26:09.550 --> 00:26:12.321
we're going to need
to authenticate it.

00:26:12.321 --> 00:26:14.820
For this, we're going to look
at that Firebase config again.

00:26:19.940 --> 00:26:22.670
See, part of that
Firebase variable

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:24.530
is the credential
that the SDK itself

00:26:24.530 --> 00:26:26.045
is using to authenticate.

00:26:26.045 --> 00:26:29.700
And that credential
serves [INAUDIBLE] tokens.

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:31.370
So you can get the
same cacheable token

00:26:31.370 --> 00:26:34.310
that we use if you're making
any requests to another Google

00:26:34.310 --> 00:26:36.980
service.

00:26:36.980 --> 00:26:41.210
Now all we have to do is
make our HTTP request.

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:45.230
We're going to send a
post to this address using

00:26:45.230 --> 00:26:48.380
the action we decided earlier,
either batch add or batch

00:26:48.380 --> 00:26:49.770
remove.

00:26:49.770 --> 00:26:52.926
To authenticate, we'll
add some headers.

00:26:52.926 --> 00:26:56.257
And then our body will be the
topic that we want to modify

00:26:56.257 --> 00:26:57.840
and the list of
tokens we want to use.

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:07.090
So this could be a full
subscription service.

00:27:07.090 --> 00:27:08.680
As an exercise for
the reader, there's

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:12.420
some other stuff you can do.

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:15.500
For example, this
method has return value

00:27:15.500 --> 00:27:18.390
and contains an array of a
list of objects that correspond

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:20.850
to either success or an
error if that token was

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:22.608
expired or invalidated.

00:27:25.240 --> 00:27:27.380
But now with our
subscription service in hand,

00:27:27.380 --> 00:27:28.905
we can try push notifications.

00:27:31.820 --> 00:27:35.360
We'll write another
database event handler

00:27:35.360 --> 00:27:37.720
where we listen
to the text field

00:27:37.720 --> 00:27:39.360
of every message in every room.

00:27:43.610 --> 00:27:47.310
Now if there was no @ mention,
which is fairly common,

00:27:47.310 --> 00:27:50.260
we can just early exit.

00:27:50.260 --> 00:27:51.887
This is a small--
or a simpler version

00:27:51.887 --> 00:27:53.470
of what we did before
where we console

00:27:53.470 --> 00:27:55.450
logged and then returned
because the return

00:27:55.450 --> 00:27:58.690
value of every function is
logged in the Google Cloud

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:00.145
logging console.

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:17.570
So next, we have the
room ID from this event.

00:28:17.570 --> 00:28:20.150
That's a stable identifier
but it's not human readable.

00:28:20.150 --> 00:28:22.760
We actually have a title
for our various rooms.

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:25.910
So we'll use the admin
SDK one more time

00:28:25.910 --> 00:28:28.860
to look up that room's
title based on its ID.

00:28:37.122 --> 00:28:39.600
Now we'll construct our payload.

00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:42.540
We're going to send the
message to a topic based

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:46.751
on the room ID and a message
that includes the friendly room

00:28:46.751 --> 00:28:47.250
name.

00:28:50.540 --> 00:28:53.170
And then we use the messaging
features inside the admin

00:28:53.170 --> 00:28:56.800
SDK to send that message.

00:28:56.800 --> 00:29:00.795
So, let's go ahead and
see it in production.

00:29:00.795 --> 00:29:07.420
[INAUDIBLE] I missed it.

00:29:07.420 --> 00:29:09.630
There we go.

00:29:09.630 --> 00:29:11.230
Serverless push notifications.

